              "text": "The question of \"how much % a mutual fund manager makes\" can refer to two different things: **the percentage fee they charge investors** to manage the fund, or **the percentage of money the individual manager personally takes home** as compensation. \n\n---\n\n### 1. What Mutual Funds Charge Investors (Management Fees)\nThis is the percentage of your investment that the fund company takes each year to manage the pool of money. This is expressed as the **expense ratio**.\n\n* **Actively Managed Mutual Funds:** Typically charge **0.5% to 1.5%** of Assets Under Management (AUM) annually. If you invest $10,000, you will pay between $50 and $150 a year to the management company.\n* **Passively Managed Mutual Funds (Index Funds):** Typically charge **0.05% to 0.25%** (and sometimes as low as 0.01% to 0.03%). Because these funds simply track an index (like the S&P 500), they require very little hands-on management.\n* **Unlike Hedge Funds:** Traditional mutual funds **do not** typically charge a performance fee (like the famous \"2 and 20\" model where hedge funds take 2% of assets and 20% of the profits). They only make a flat percentage of the total assets, regardless of whether the fund goes up or down.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Individual Fund Managers Personally Earn\nIf you are asking how much the actual human portfolio manager (PM) personally makes, they do not just pocket the 1% fee. They are employees of an asset management firm (such as Fidelity, Vanguard, or BlackRock) and are compensated through a mix of base salary and performance bonuses.\n\n* **As a percentage of the fund\u2019s AUM:** While structures vary, a lead portfolio manager\u2019s total compensation package often equates to roughly **0.10% to 0.20% of the fund\u2019s total assets**. \n  * *Example:* If a manager oversees a **$1 billion** active mutual fund charging a 1% fee, the fund generates $10 million in revenue. After paying for operations, legal, marketing, and research analysts, the lead portfolio manager might take home **$1 million to $2 million** (all-in) for the year.\n* **As a percentage of performance (P&L):** Roughly 80% of mutual fund managers have their bonuses explicitly tied to performance. However, instead of taking a flat percentage of profits (like the 15% to 20% P&L cut that hedge fund managers receive), they are measured against a benchmark (like the S&P 500) over a 3-year rolling period. If they beat the benchmark, their bonus increases; if they underperform, it shrinks.\n* **Average Total Compensation:** For a standard equity portfolio manager at a mid-to-large-size mutual fund firm, total annual compensation (salary + bonus) typically ranges from **$400,000 to over $1.5 million** depending on the size of the fund and their track record. Managers of mega-funds (managing $10 billion+) can make tens of millions, but they represent a small fraction of the industry.",
